short-code {"wall_s": 4.561758000000001, "ttft_s": 0.46239545900000145, "text": "```python\nimport unittest\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\n\n\nclass Node:\n    \"\"\"Doubly linked list node for LRU cache.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, key: Any = None, value: Any = None):\n        self.key = key\n        self.value = value\n        self.prev: Optional['Node'] = None\n        self.next: Optional['Node'] = None\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    LRU Cache implementation using a doubly linked list and a dictionary.\n    \n    The doubly linked list maintains the order of usage (most recently used at head,\n    least recently used at tail). The dictionary provides O(1) lookup for nodes.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be positive\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache: dict[Any, Node] = {}\n        # Dummy head and tail nodes to simplify edge cases\n        self.head = Node()  # Most recently used side\n        self.tail = Node()  # Least recently used side\n        self.head.next = self.tail\n        self.tail.prev = self.head\n    \n    def _", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 68, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 324, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 68}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-code", "thinking": false}
short-story {"wall_s": 5.185853417000001, "ttft_s": 0.30040358300000136, "text": "The fog didn\u2019t roll in; it collapsed. One moment the Atlantic was a churning sheet of black glass, the next, it was a wall of wet wool pressing against the lantern room\u2019s thick panes.\n\nElias wiped his hands on a rag that had seen better decades. He was seventy-two, his joints clicking like the rusted gears of the clockwork mechanism he kept wound every four hours. The light swept out, a rhythmic *thrum-thrum* that vibrated in his teeth.\n\nThen, the radio crackled. Not the static hiss of interference, but a voice. Clear. Dry. Terrifyingly familiar.\n\n\"Elias. Don\u2019t wind the clock.\"\n\nElias froze, his hand hovering over the brass crank. He knew that voice. It was his own, stripped of the gravel and age, sharp with panic.\n\n\"Who is this?\" Elias shouted into the receiver, though he knew the answer. The frequency was dead air. No one used this channel.\n\n\"Listen to me,\" the voice said, breathless. \"The storm isn\u2019t a storm. It\u2019s a breach. If you wind the clock, the light stays on. If the light stays on, *they* see the door.", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 69, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 325, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 69}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-story", "thinking": false}
short-reasoning {"wall_s": 4.561994332999998, "ttft_s": 0.3207925419999995, "text": "To determine the number of workers needed, we must calculate the effective capacity per worker after accounting for the safety reserve, and then divide the total load by this effective capacity.\n\n### Step 1: Determine Effective Capacity per Worker\n\nEach worker can handle **20 requests per second (RPS)** at full capacity. However, we must reserve **25%** of this capacity for spikes. This means only **75%** of the worker's capacity is available for normal traffic.\n\n$$\n\\text{Effective Capacity per Worker} = 20 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 20 \\times 0.75 = 15 \\text{ RPS}\n$$\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Required Workers\n\nThe service receives **120 RPS**. We divide the total load by the effective capacity per worker:\n\n$$\n\\text{Workers Needed} = \\frac{\\text{Total Load}}{\\text{Effective Capacity per Worker}} = \\frac{120}{15}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{120}{15} = 8\n$$\n\nSince the result is a whole number, no rounding up is necessary.\n\n### Step 3:", "reasoning": "", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 86, "completion_tokens": 256, "total_tokens": 342, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 86}}, "finish": "length", "done": true, "temperature": 0, "case": "short-reasoning", "thinking": false}
